<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Datames</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 362 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Datames was a Carian-born Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Cappadocia and one of the ablest generals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in the 4th century BCE. He captured the rebellious Paphlagonian ruler Thys and fought for Artaxerxes II, but later revolted against him, taking part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-satraps-revolt">Great Satraps' Revolt</a>. He was murdered by an agent of the king around 362 BCE. Cornelius Nepos wrote his biography.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/datames.jpg" alt="Datames" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_Datames.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Datames was born around 407 BCE, the son of Camisares, an Iranian satrap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> under Artaxerxes II who was most likely from a Persian noble family settled in Caria, and of Scythissa, a Paphlagonian princess. His name is the Hellenized form of an Old Iranian name, from either Dātamiθra, gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>, or Data-ama, to whom force is given; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> it appears as Tadanmu. He was also known as Tarkamuwa, a name of Luwian origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Datames became one of the king's bodyguards and distinguished himself in the war against the Cadusii, in which his father fell, and he was appointed to succeed him in the government of his province. He brought back under the king two officials who had revolted, Thyus, governor of Paphlagonia, and Aspis of Cataonia. Artaxerxes then gave him the chief command of a force to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after the failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnabazus-ii">Pharnabazus II</a>, but the intrigues of his enemies at court and the risks they brought led him to throw off his allegiance around 370 BCE. He withdrew to Cappadocia and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-satraps-revolt">Satraps' Revolt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Artabazos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespontine-phrygia">Hellespontine Phrygia</a>, advanced against him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a> and was entirely defeated, and Autophradates, sent with a large army, had to retreat with heavy losses. After evading many plots, Datames was assassinated at a conference by Mithridates, son of Ariobarzanes, who had won his confidence by pretending to be hostile to the king. His son Ariamnes I succeeded him as satrap of Cappadocia.</p><p>Cornelius Nepos, whose sketch is the only connected narrative of his life, ranked him the bravest and ablest of all non-Greek and non-Roman generals except Hamilcar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. The accounts are confused, however, the anecdotes recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a> are hard to place, and the chronology is obscure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Datames” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thys defied Persia until <b>Datames</b> captured him c. 380 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-paphlagonia">Kingdom of Paphlagonia</a></li></ul></section>
